  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes the offshore deep-sea mineral extraction process and its main environmental pressures, while contrasting mining with exploration. On the mining side, a support vessel sits above a seafloor mining machine operating at roughly 4–6.5 km depth. Material is lifted to the ship through a hydraulic system, and ore is transferred away by another vessel. The diagram highlights several impact pathways: greenhouse-gas emissions from ships, disposal of water and fine sediments after onboard dewatering, and large particle plumes spreading through the water and along the seabed. Near the mining vehicle, the seafloor is physically disturbed, with labels indicating habitat loss, burial of organisms by sedimentation, and organism death from sediment compaction. A possible release of toxic metals is also suggested in the plume region.\n\nThe left side shows practical site constraints, such as avoiding steep slopes and underwater obstacles like lava flows. On the exploration side, a research vessel uses tools such as multibeam echo systems, ROVs, AUVs, and box corers to map and sample the seabed. Overall, the figure presents the full deep-sea mining chain together with its visible ecological and emission-related risks.",
